---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import { routeFigures } from '../data/figures'
import { introSections, join, routeSections, siteRoutes } from '../data/sections'

/* Static Astro, no island, no `client:` directive and no chart: the front door is prose, so
 * #36's server-render rule is satisfied vacuously and greppably. */

/* This page's own sections live in src/data/sections.ts as `introSections`, not in this
 * frontmatter, so /contents lists exactly what the front door renders. They are still deliberately
 * NOT an entry in `routeSections`: that map's keys are the domain of a glossary term's
 * `first_used.route`, and a term whose first prose use is the front door is a category error,
 * this page names no term. See the header of src/data/sections.ts. */

/* Every count on this page is read from the same arrays and the same collection the rest of the
 * site renders from, never typed here: a route that gains or loses a section, a figure or a term
 * updates this page in the same build, so the two cannot drift. */
const siteSectionCount = siteRoutes.reduce((n, r) => n + r.sections.length, 0)
const siteFigureCount = Object.values(routeFigures).reduce((n, f) => n + f.length, 0)
const termCount = (await getCollection('glossary')).length

const cards = [
  {
    href: join('/economy'),
    name: 'Economy',
    answers:
      'What the country produces, who works, what things cost, and how the proceeds divide between labor and capital.',
    meta: `Route 1 of 3 · ${routeSections['/economy'].length} sections`,
  },
  {
    href: join('/households'),
    name: 'Households',
    answers:
      'What a household earns, what the code says it owes, and what it pays once every federal tax is counted.',
    meta: `Route 2 of 3 · ${routeSections['/households'].length} sections`,
  },
  {
    href: join('/government'),
    name: 'Government',
    answers:
      'The debt, who holds it, the budget behind it, and the laws that moved it, with cost and recorded vote.',
    meta: `Route 3 of 3 · ${routeSections['/government'].length} sections`,
  },
  {
    href: join('/contents'),
    name: 'Contents',
    answers:
      'Every route, every section, every numbered figure and every defined term, on one page, with a link to each.',
    meta: `Reference · ${siteSectionCount} sections, ${siteFigureCount} figures, ${termCount} terms`,
  },
  {
    href: join('/sources'),
    name: 'Sources',
    answers:
      'Where every number on the site came from, named in full, and where two official series disagree with each other.',
    meta: 'Reference · every source, with vintage and known discrepancies',
  },
  {
    href: join('/glossary'),
    name: 'Glossary',
    answers:
      'What a technical term means here, in one sentence, with the fuller entry underneath it.',
    meta: 'Reference · every technical term the routes use, defined once',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Income & Tax | US federal finance"
  description="The American economy and government in numbers."
  sections={introSections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Introduction</p>
  <h1>Income &amp; Tax</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">the American economy and government in numbers</p>

  <section id="what-this-is">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>What this site is, and what it refuses to do</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      This site accounts for where the federal balance sheet came from, told only from primary
      data. The data is decades of federal budget figures. The set covers every major
      deficit-moving law, with its cost and its recorded vote. The same sources name who holds the
      debt and where the revenue comes from. They also compare American taxes with those of other
      rich countries.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The recurring finding concerns the popular story, the one in which one party did this. That
      story does not hold against the data. Divided control covers most of the period. Most of the
      largest deficit-moving laws passed with votes from both parties. And a large share of the
      accumulated deficits is net interest, a line nobody votes on at all. Each of those claims is
      made on a route, with the evidence and the source beside it. None of it is quoted here.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      That is the refusal. Every figure here names its source in full and states the units on both
      of its axes. The numbers behind the picture are in the page, for anyone who wants to check
      the drawing against them. Where the data cannot answer a question, the route says so instead
      of estimating.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="brief-quote">
      <p>
        The site's job is to let a reader check that for themselves. Persuading the reader is not
        the job.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="brief-attrib">The site's design brief</p>
  </section>

  <section id="where-to-start">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 2</span></p>
    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Six destinations, in two kinds. The three routes present the analysis. The three reference
      pages hold the sources, the contents and the definitions.
    </p>
    <ol class="cards">
      {cards.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <h3><a href={c.href}>{c.name}</a></h3>
          <p class="prose">{c.answers}</p>
          <p class="card-meta">{c.meta}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="prose">
      The three routes are meant to be read in order. The economy sets the denominators the other
      two are measured against. Households is where the tax code meets a person. The three
      reference pages are for looking something up, at any point. None of them assumes you have
      read anything else.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-read-a-figure">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 3</span></p>
    <h2>How to read a figure</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Every figure on this site is wrapped in the same apparatus, in the same order, on every
      route. The apparatus is worth a minute up front, because it is what makes a figure checkable
      rather than merely convincing.
    </p>

    <h3>The ladder above the chart</h3>
    <p class="prose">
      Each section opens the same way, with a section number, a heading, a standfirst and then a
      finding. The standfirst says what the section is about. The finding is one sentence, ruled
      off above and below, stating the single claim the chart is there to support. The finding
      comes before the picture on purpose. Read it, then look at the chart, and check whether the
      picture says what the sentence says. If the two disagree, the sentence is wrong, and that is
      worth knowing.
    </p>

    <h3>The caption beneath it</h3>
    <p class="prose">
      Under the chart sit up to three lines, always in this order.
    </p>
    <dl class="apparatus">
      <dt><span class="lead">Units.</span></dt>
      <dd>What each axis measures, the horizontal one first, in the units the numbers are in.</dd>
      <dt><span class="lead">Note.</span></dt>
      <dd>
        A scope or method warning. A projection drawn beyond the last actual year, or a series
        that changes definition partway. Present only when there is one to give.
      </dd>
      <dt><span class="lead">Source.</span></dt>
      <dd>The agency, the dataset and its vintage, as the dataset itself states them.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="prose">
      Units come first because a number without its unit is not checkable. The choice of unit is
      also where this subject is most often distorted. The source line is rendered verbatim and is
      never summarised down to the name of an agency. A figure whose source is missing fails the
      build. So does a figure whose axes do not both name a unit.
    </p>

    <h3>View as table</h3>
    <p class="prose">
      Beneath each chart is a closed disclosure holding the same numbers the chart draws. The
      disclosure holds a real table in the served HTML, present whether or not scripting is
      enabled. It is not a screen-reader-only copy. Anyone can open it, read the values, and check
      the drawing against them.
    </p>

    <h3>The unit toggle</h3>
    <p class="prose">
      Where a series can honestly be shown in more than one unit, the figure carries a toggle. The
      three settings are Nominal, Real FY2025 and % of GDP. The same series tells three different
      stories in the three. A line that climbs steeply in nominal dollars can be close to flat once
      inflation is taken out. Set against the size of the economy, the same line can be falling.
      All three of those are true at once. Choosing one silently is the commonest way this subject
      gets distorted. The choice therefore stays yours, and the caption always states which one is
      showing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="where-the-numbers-come-from">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 4</span></p>
    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Every figure on this site traces to a named source with a vintage. Four separate build steps
      enforce that. Each one fails if a source is missing.
    </p>
    <ol class="prose">
      <li>
        A dataset with no source is never published. The step that writes the site's data files
        refuses to write one that does not name its origin.
      </li>
      <li>
        The same omission fails validation. A separate check re-reads every published file and
        fails it if the source is missing. The same check fails a source shortened to name an
        agency but no dataset.
      </li>
      <li>
        The site refuses to load an unsourced dataset. Every data file is asserted as the site
        starts building. One missing its source throws before a single page is rendered.
      </li>
      <li>
        A figure that omits its source, or either axis unit, cannot build. The figure component
        itself throws, so a chart cannot reach a reader without its apparatus.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="prose">
      The register itself is on <a href={join('/sources')}>Sources</a>, where each entry carries
      its vintage and any known discrepancy between official series. The vocabulary is on
      <a href={join('/glossary')}>Glossary</a>, where every technical term the routes use is
      defined once.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
